  {
    "episode_id": 91204,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91204.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "The influence of campaign money inside the Congress, as opposed to within political parties, has transformed this place from when I first arrived. And it is an insidious growth inside the institution, which pits member against member, where you have to raise funds to defeat your colleagues. And when it started, oh, 25 years ago, it wasn't very perceptible because people didn't really do it. But it grew like a cancer inside to where now I can guarantee you, if members are honest, those from marginal districts are raising $3 million plus for their next election. And the other party, whoever the other party is, is doing the same thing. And so what happens is the focus turns to fundraising. Now, you can say, well, what does that have to do? You know, there's not necessarily a connection between fundraising and what people vote on here. But I'll tell you, number one, it's distracting. It takes some people are spending over half their time on fundraising,",
