    "turn_text": "in one major bill working its way through that has to do with public financing, there is an opt out. Right. So it'll just encourage more big money people to run for office. And the system will skew more toward electing the very wealthy. And I have a bill that actually creates a constitutional amendment to overturn the decision of the Supreme Court, called Buckley versus Vallejo, that says that money is speech. In other words, well, you can't try to limit any spending or contributions because you would deny free speech. Sounds like logical argument. Well, but what if you don't have the money? Then you don't have free speech. How do we then have court cases being generated around this country to overturn Buckley versus Vallejo? My bill would do that and allow the federal government and the state to set contribution and spending limits.",
