  {
    "episode_id": 91204,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91204.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "we met with Chairman Bernanke in a private meeting and he paints this rosy picture. I sat there and listened to him. I couldn't believe what he was saying. And I said, let me tell you what I just experienced this weekend. I said, today I woke up in my home district. The newspaper said our medical university was laying off 50 people out there at the medical college. I met with a businesswoman yesterday whose friend, very reputable local business, had tried to get a $65,000 loan. $65,000 could not get it from a local bank. Credit is not flowing. despite what he said. I talked about the street that I'd been on this weekend in one of the middle-class areas of the town that I live in in Toledo. That is a solid middle-class neighborhood. So many homes were for sale on that street, and I was trying to communicate that to him. I talked to a friend in Las Vegas, Nevada, who's out of work the other night, and asking about, this is someone who lost their home. and told me, Marcy, it's bad out here. And after December, when the Convention Center gets built, there's going to be another 4,000 people out of work out here. And I talked to the Chairman Bernanke about how many foreclosures were increasing around the country and urged him to use his voice inside the administration to look at a program of turnkey rental. where you could have the current residents be caretakers for that property, pay what they could based on what they're earning right now, but not just to leave all these vacant homes around the country where the hemorrhage continues, and then they get turned over through the big banks to these repossession companies which don't maintain the homes. Who does that help for heaven's sake? And I told them that 80% of the homes that are being sold in so many of our communities with high unemployment are going to outside investors again who don't maintain the properties. He and I live in two different worlds. He looked at me and he didn't have much of a response. He said, well, you know, a lot of the solutions lie within the hands of Congress. But the credit situation belongs to him. He regulates the banks for heaven's sake to the Federal Reserve. I couldn't believe kind of the tepid. Go along to get along a response that I got. And I thought, you know, if Roosevelt were president, he'd get his arms around those banks in a week. He would bring these people in and he would begin to turn America around, not just wait for the market to lumber along and do whatever it's going to do through the winter months. I thought, wow, what a difference between a man named Bernard Baruch, who really understood what to do with Wall Street in the economy when he served President Roosevelt and what we have today. And I sort of left that mean thinking, He's one of the people in charge, but he's not. He's not making the system better. And I'm not saying he's a bad person or that he isn't very polished in his presentation, but he was unsympathetic.",
