    "turn_idx": 86,
    "turn_text": "off some of the public because they didn't really understand what it was. They thought it meant they were going to lose their insurance and I never cared for that word. We're talking about the right of people to buy insurance even if they have preexisting conditions that they can't be denied. If you're a small business that you'll have access to buy a plan and you'll get some help from the government in the form of a tax credit or rebate If you can't afford it, we're talking about accessibility to insurance and affordability. In my district, where there's an organization called Front Path, I encourage all your listeners to go to their website and look at it, what it is, is a public option without the government. In the sense that 140,000 people who are insured back together, business, labor, government at the local level, small business, And they negotiate price with those that provided hospitals, doctors, durable medical equipment, pharmaceutical companies. They are able to contain costs because they have buying power. That's what you want in the regional health market. I'm trying so hard to get that built into this bill. What that does is that we're implemented across the country. That would save money from coast to coast. through these regional group purchasing pools. And you would be able to provide insurance to all. So I think the goal is for everybody to have insurance. And I'm not sure that that word communicates what we want. So somehow it's like we've gotten locked into the rhetoric and so we stopped thinking about alternatives to provide this good care at affordable prices for all.",
