    "turn_text": "only 11 such prototypes in the nation. And what it's like, it's not a co-op, but it's sort of like a credit union, but it's a consumer's buying pool. They are remarkable. And they put nurses on staff. They're not an insurance plan. You pay $4 a month. They're not for profit. All right? And what they do is they band together and then they negotiate. They negotiate with docs, with hospitals, with durable medical equipment, with pharmaceuticals. And they have enough buying power that they have a weight in the marketplace. That's much better than asking a small business to go out there. Well, you go paddle your little canoe in the insurance market. You get the best rate you can. You're not alone. They're the fulcrum in the health marketplace and they really can bend the cost curve long term. They've proved it. And they have nurses on staff. They call you. They say, have you had your gynecological exams this year for men? Have you had your prostate screenings? They affirmatively work with beneficiaries and they have been extraordinarily successful. So I think there's different ways to get what we want and not get mixed up in the rhetoric. and to give people power",
